  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1112 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft is still dominated by a massive high\npressure system over the southeastern portion of the CONUS. A \nlow pressure system continues to deepen dramatically just off of\nthe Pacific Northwest. A very weak axis of low pressure persist\nin western Kansas. Despite this, convection has not \nmaterialized in Kansas. Some very weak showers have developed in\neastern Colorado, but even if it clips the CWA in the coming \nhours the impact will be minimal. \n\nHeat continues to be the headline over the coming days. A Heat \nAdvisory is in effect from now until 8 PM Sunday. This included the \nHighway 183 corridor and eastward. Even if the criteria is not met \nat an apparent temperature of 105 degrees, the prolonged span of \nheat warrants the advisory. Highs Friday will primarily be around \n100-102 degrees with apparent temperatures up 107 degrees in \nthe eastern counties. Outside of the blistering heat, the other \nfocus of the forecast period is the storm chances Friday and \nSaturday. Both days, CAMs continue to show inconsistent signals \nof convection. Especially Friday is still progged to be \nprimarily scattered storms. Just enough environmental \ningredients in NAM forecast soundings (1200 J/kg CAPE, 30 KTs \nshear, and low level lapse rates of 10 C/km). If the storms \norganize, the strongest could approach severe criteria with 50+ \nmph wind gusts and penny sized hail. That said the expectation \nis that the storms will remain well below severe levels. The \nstorms will form in western Kansas and out of Colorado around 5 \nPM. How long and how far east the storms make it is under \nsubstantial CAM disagreement. Tomorrow's forecast will have a \nbetter grasp on the situation. Relative humidity minimums are \nforecast to stay away of headline criteria at above 20%. However\nwith wind gusts of up to 20-25 mph out of the south, elevated \nconditions may develop in far western Kansas in the late \nafternoon. \n\nSaturday is currently forecast to be a couple degrees cooler, but \nstill around the triple digit range. The storm potential will nearly \nperfectly replicate Friday. Storms developing and moving in from \nColorado in the early evening with an uncertain lifespan and \neastward extent. Forecast soundings suggest there may be more CAPE \nand less shear available. Without the same amount of shear, the \nsevere chances (that were already low) drop significantly. This\nis still subject to change in future forecasts, but just how \nthe storms are looking currently. \n\nSunday through Tuesday will continue to be hot with highs in the \nupper 90s, but with even less chances for precipitation. With \nrelative humidities staying above 20%, no headlines are expected \nover that stretch after the Heat Advisory expiring Sunday. The past \ncouple days, ensembles have been bullish on Wednesday's \nprecipitation. These chances have dropped significantly. What \noriginally appeared like a widespread 20-40% opportunity is now only \na 10-20% chance and reserved for the northeast zones. More \nmonitoring will be needed especially if these drier trends\ncontinue.",
